+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **BSP Tree Depth:** The BSP tree can become deep and unbalanced, leading to $O(b)$ cost per search and potentially $O(b^2)$ overall flavor if not balanced [S1][S7, unofficial].
+*   **PPC List Growth:** If mesh elements "spike in" behind others, the PPC list can grow. However, for well-shaped meshes, it remains small [S5].
+*   **Topological Data:** If the input lacks adjacency pointers, preprocessing takes $O(n \log n)$ [S5].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time and memory complexity:**
-*   The time complexity is $O(n + bp)$, where $n$ is the number of cells, $b$ is the number of exterior faces, and $p$ is the size of a small subset of exterior cells [S1][S4, S5, unofficial].
-*   Memory complexity is dominated by the storage of the BSP tree, which is proportional to the number of exterior faces $b$ [S4, S5, unofficial].
-
-**Measured performance:**
-*   BSP-XMPVO results are orders of magnitude better than previous exact methods of sorting cells [S2, S3, S4, unofficial].
-*   It leads to an order of magnitude improvement in sorting times over XMPVO [S1][S4, S5, unofficial].
-
-**Scaling:**
-*   The performance depends on the number of exterior faces $b$. If the BSP tree becomes deep and unbalanced, the cost per search can degrade to $O(b)$, leading to an overall $O(b^2)$ flavor in practical cases [S1][S4, S5, unofficial].
-
-**Strengths and limitations:**
-*   **Strengths:** Provides exact visibility ordering for general unstructured meshes, significantly faster than previous exact methods [S2, S4, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** The preprocessing step (building the BSP tree) is very slow and can add considerable processing overhead. The algorithm can become sluggish if the BSP tree becomes deep and unbalanced [S1][S4, S5, unofficial].
-
-**When to use it and when not:**
-Not given in the sources.
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(n + bp)$ per viewpoint, where $n$ is total cells, $b$ is boundary cells, and $p$ is the number of partially projected cells [S1, S5][S7, unofficial]. This is an improvement over XMPVO's $O(b^2 + n)$ [S5].
+*   **Preprocessing Time:** Building the BSP tree is slow. For a 13,000 cell dataset, it took 3.0 seconds; for 190,000 cells, 6.4 seconds; for 240,000 cells, 5.8 seconds [S5].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Linear in the number of cells and boundary facets. The BSP tree size is $\Theta(b^2)$ worst-case but near-linear $O(b)$ in practice [S5].
+*   **Performance:** On a 13,000 cell dataset, BSP-XMPVO took 0.37 seconds (vs 3.5s for XMPVO, 0.07s for MPVONC). On 190,000 cells, 2.5s (vs 25s for XMPVO). On 240,000 cells, 2.9s (vs 48s for XMPVO) [S5].
+*   **Strengths:** Exact sorting for non-convex meshes; significantly faster than XMPVO; near-linear runtime for typical meshes.
+*   **Limitations:** Slow preprocessing; BSP tree can be unbalanced; PPC dependencies add overhead if the mesh has many non-convex interlocking structures [S1, S5][S7, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** Interactive rendering of unstructured grids where exact visibility is required for non-convex meshes and preprocessing cost is acceptable [S5].
+*   **When Not to Use:** If the mesh is convex (use MPVO for speed); if preprocessing time is prohibitive; if the mesh has extreme non-convexities causing large PPC lists or deep BSP trees.
 
 ## Background
-BSP-XMPVO was proposed by João Luiz Dihl Comba, James T. Klosowski, Nelson Max, and Peter L. Williams in 1999 [S2, S3, S5, unofficial]. It was introduced as an improvement on the XMPVO method by Silva et al., which itself was an extension of MPVO by Williams [S2, S3, S4, S5, unofficial]. The method was published in the journal *Computers & Graphics* (or *Computer Graphics Forum*, as indicated by the source title) in September 1999 [S2, S3, unofficial]. It addresses the limitations of MPVO, which only provides approximate ordering for general datasets, and XMPVO, which, while exact, can be slower due to dynamic ray shooting [S2, S4, S5, unofficial].
+BSP-XMPVO was proposed by Comba et al. in 1999 [S5]. It builds on MPVO by Williams (1992) and XMPVO by Silva et al. [S5]. It was designed to close the gap between rendering regular and irregular grids by providing fast, exact visibility ordering for unstructured grids [S5].
 
